SM(312,{"n":"Exodus 29.1-14","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Exodus 29.1-14.mp3","d":2533.0,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.36,"So we'll go ahead and get started when we get started here in just a moment."],[24.0,"We'll be picking up the beginning of Exodus chapter 29."],[28.64,"Last time, of course, we talked about Chapter 28."],[32.56,"That's how these things work."],[35.68,"But chapter 28 was about."],[39.12,"So before chapter 28, we were talking about the construction, the design, the implementation of the tabernacle."],[47.28,"Uh, and the structures and veils and everything involved in the tabernacle."],[51.6,"Then chapter 28 was talking about the vestments of the high priests and of the priests."],[59.36,"As sort of a continuation of that"],[63.0,"And remember, all of this that we're reading is in the context of Moses is on top of the mountain talking to God."],[72.2,"And so we talked about how this is sort of being revealed to him, and that he's designing these things after the pattern of what he sees on the mountain in the presence of God."],[86.09,"And that this is what Hebrews that we read through a couple years ago was is pointing at when it talks about the things on earth being a shadow of like the heavenly sanctuary."],[98.49,"People want to say that's like this Platonist thing."],[101.61,"But no, that's we've seen here in Exodus that he these things are a copy."],[106.69,"They're following the pattern."],[108.43,"And that's what Hebrews is then pointing back to."],[113.15,"So what we're gonna get into tonight, logically, uh follows after the vestments."],[125.47,"And so tonight uh God is gonna lay out the process for consecrating the high priest and priests."],[134.51,"Uh, in this case, Aaron and his and his sons."],[138.99,"So we're gonna start at least."],[140.51,"We'll see how far we get."],[141.63,"But that's where we're going in chapter 29."],[144.27,"Um, so we can have a quick recap there because obviously I'm not. going into detail about the vestments again, but that's what was set up last time"],[152.31,"And so now it's right, we sort of worked our way down."],[155.76,"Remember, we started out in the description, it started out describing the Ark of the Covenant and the most holy place in the tabernacle."],[162.88,"And remember we work sort of worked our way out to the outer courts in the description, and then we moved from there to the vestments of the priests, and now we're at the ordination or the consecration of the priests."],[178.76,"Of this larger section."],[182.36,"So, unless there are any leftover questions or comments or insights about the Boar War, we'll go ahead and pick up in Exodus chapter 29, verse 1."],[196.28,"Now, this is what you shall do to them to consecrate them for ministering to me as priests to serve me in the priesthood."],[204.12,"Take one young bull and two rams without blemish, and unleavened loaves kneaded with oil, and unleavened cakes anointed with oil."],[213.96,"You shall make them of wheat flour, you shall put them in one basket, and bring them in the basket with the young bull and the two rams."],[223.68,"So you probably immediately picked up when the animals were named that this is for a sacrifice that's going to be offered, in the context of this ordination."],[239.7,"So this ordination takes place within the context of sacrificial ritual."],[247.54,"And we're going to see all of these kinds of what we would call sacramental things."],[255.3,"And sacramental things is actually repetitious."],[258.66,"It's like saying ATM machine."],[262.74,"sacrament sacred thing. So sayingal things is sort of saying things"],[273.7,"But these sacred things."],[276.02,"These processes, these different things, are joined with the sacrificial worship that is the center of the worship and relationship between Israel and her gods."],[289.62,"And so within the context of that, these other things, these other moments happen."],[295.46,"Okay. And this is especially important when we're thinking about ornation consecration of the high priest and the priests, because what is their job going to be?"],[307.14,"Their job is gonna be to make sa be the ones who make sacrificial offerings on behalf of themselves and the people."],[314.44,"They're gonna be the ones who are actually going in and offering the incense, actually offering the animals."],[320.53,"In addition to preparing and butchering the animals."],[324.02,"So the ordination or consecration here is connected with them making their first sacrificial offering."],[335.84,"That's the core of what's going on."],[339.2,"Another note. And this is an important note."],[342.18,"This is going to be the first time, but definitely not the last time as we go through the rest of Exodus at a de Leviticus that I'm going to make this point."],[350.1,"But you may have noticed that not all of these offerings are animals."],[357.12,"We've got some animals."],[358.32,"We've got a young bull and two rams, but then we've also got unleavened loaves kneaded with oil and unleavened cakes anointed with oil."],[367.6,"We have cakes, loaves"],[372.92,"We're gonna see a lot of the sacrificial offerings in Israel, and this is not unique to Israel."],[380.36,"This is true across the board in the ancient Near East, the ancient world in general, and sacrifice."],[386.04,"A lot of the offerings are going to be cakes of various kinds."],[391.08,"And what those cakes and loaves and things are is gonna vary from culture to culture for kind of obvious reasons."],[398.77,"In terms of what grains are available in different parts of the world."],[402.19,"So you're gonna get a lot of like wheat cakes inoman religion, R"],[406.89,"I imagine if you look at sacrificial ritual, I haven't made a huge study of sacrificial ritual in Chinese history, but I imagine you're looking more rice cakes."],[415.11,"Because that's the kind of flour they're gonna have to work with."],[417.69,"So as you get different grains in different places, you're gonna have different."],[421.77,"But the idea of offering breads, cakes, loaves, this is a this is a constant."],[429.42,"Isn't the underlying thing food?"],[431.34,"I mean, it is food."],[434.94,"So everything that gets offered is in this category of food, in sacrifices."],[442.03,"But not everything gets killed."],[447.07,"Not everything has blood."],[451.19,"I'm going to be emphasizing this all the way through because in our modern contemporary understanding of things."],[460.2,"We've been taught that sacrificing means killing."],[465.98,"And that the important part of sacrificial rituals is the blood, shedding blood, spilling blood."],[473.63,"Well, you can't kill uh a wheat cake, and it doesn't have any blood if you stab it."],[482.63,"And we're gonna see when we get into Leviticus, a lot of the sin offerings are wheat cake."],[487.37,"So we're gonna have more conversations about this."],[489.75,"But it's important to notice this."],[492.84,"And we're gonna see those sacrifices."],[495.08,"Most of of the time, other than the Day Atonement, which we'll read about in detail in Leviticus, nothing special is done with the blood."],[504.48,"They drain the blood out of the animal and they pour it into the ground at the base of the altar."],[510.96,"They just dispose of it."],[513.5,"Because God's gonna say, don't do anything with the blood."],[516.3,"Because the pagans would do, they drink the blood, they do all kinds of rituals with the blood."],[522.82,"And God says, no, you drain it, you get rid of it."],[529.14,"But what you have always, as we're gonna see as we go through the Torah through the Pentateuch, it's sacrificial rituals is, it's always food."],[538.42,"There's a portion of it that's offered to God by burning it."],[543.1,"Generally the best portion."],[545.63,"Best portions. There's a portion that's eaten by the priests."],[549.79,"We've already seen that in Exodus."],[551.47,"It's already talked about that a little bit."],[553.65,"And there's a portion that's eaten by the people bringing the offering."],[560.13,"We saw that with Passover, too."],[561.81,"Remember, Passover is about eating the lamb."],[566.63,"You actually eat it, you cook it and you eat it."],[570.04,"Um. So yeah, so this is part of sort of reorienting ourselves out of that bad contemporary thing we've been fed."],[580.6,"Pun, not intended."],[584.8,"That sacrifice is all about blood, and sacrifice is all about killing things."],[590.97,"And this is also part of the some of our friends in other Christian groups who don't understand why the Eucharist is a sacrifice."],[602.65,"They can't see it."],[603.85,"That's part of why."],[606.51,"And if you tell them the Eucharist is a sacrifice, they'll say, oh, so you're killing Jesus again?"],[610.67,"And it's like, no, sacrifices aren't about killing."],[616.45,"And a lot of these sacrifices were what?"],[620.77,"Bread. So to, anyone from the ancient world."],[626.85,"So we go, we get a TARDIS, we go back to the ancient world, we grab."],[630.7,"Random ancient Near Eastern person and have them watch me do the liturgy."],[637.63,"They wouldn't know what was going on."],[638.99,"I'm talking about somebody we don't even have to teach them English."],[641.23,"They could just watch what I was doing."],[644.05,"And they wouldn't know all the ins and out the details."],[646.29,"But if you asked them, okay, what was that, they would say he was sacrificing bread and wine to his God."],[655.95,"It would just be obvious to them that that's what's going on."],[659.49,"They wouldn't know why."],[660.65,"They wouldn't know about the body and blood of Christ, obviously."],[663.05,"They wouldn't know."],[663.82,"All of that other stuff they wouldn't know about."],[665.94,"But they would look at it and say, oh yeah, that's a yeah."],[670.76,"If we just took an Israelite or a Jewish person who knew the Torah, they'd they you'd know more than that."],[676.76,"They'd say he was making a thank offering to God with bread and wine."],[682.14,"Again, wouldn't know all the ins and outs because they don't know about Christ."],[686.67,"But they know what was going on."],[689.89,"It's that obvious what's going on."],[693.29,"Um. But that's right from the beginning here in sacrifices."],[697.21,"And there's not some distinction drawn."],[700.43,"Like these animals are the sacrifices."],[703.39,"And then these cakes are like appetizers."],[707.87,"Or these cakes are"],[709.37,"There's there's actually a term called trapezomata, uh, which you use the Greek term because it sounds better than table things, which is what it literally means."],[719.23,"In sacrificial rituals, often um like pagan temples would have banquet halls."],[725.07,"And so, in addition to the sacrificed meat, they'd have what's called trapezomata, which was literally like appetizers and like rolls."],[732.67,"So, yeah, like sides."],[734.53,"Um, but that's not what this is."],[736.93,"This is part of the right, that's why God gives instructions."],[740.7,"You're offering these three animals and these two types of of cakes."],[747.58,"Verse 4. Then you shall bring Aaron and his sons. to the doors of the tabernacle of testimony and wash them with water"],[756.1,"Then taking the garments, you shall dress Aaron, your brother, with the cloak, the ephod, the full-length robe, and the oracle, and you shall attach the oracle on the ephod for him."],[766.5,"Okay, so you remember the oracle thing."],[768.74,"We won't go through all the weird translation stuff in the Orthodox Study Bible again, but that's the breastplate, is what's being called the oracle here."],[778.58,"So it's basically each layer that we talked about last time."],[782.04,"So they're dressing him."],[785.26,"So he's washed, cleaned, and then he is dressed."],[793.31,"So a couple connections here to notice."],[797.11,"This follows the pattern of what we do in baptism."],[800.73,"When we baptize somebody."],[802.98,"We baptize them and immediately afterward they're dressed, in white."],[810.4,"Um also this is the process."],[813.76,"If you've been to an ordination in the Orthodox Church or seen one on video, part of the process is the person who's getting ordained is dressed with the different pieces of the vestments."],[827.22,"By the bishop and the right."],[829.3,"And so it's the same based on the same process."],[841.02,"Verse 6, you shall put the mitre on his head and the sanctified golden plate on the mitre."],[845.74,"We talked about those last time."],[847.42,"You shall take the anointing oil, pour it on his head, and anoint him."],[852.06,"Then you shall bring his sons and put the robes on them."],[857.1,"You shall gird them with sashes, Aaron and his sons, and crest them with turbans."],[861.74,"Their priesthood to me shall be for a continual ordinance."],[865.34,"So you shall consecrate Aaron and his sons."],[869.22,"Does turban seem what we want to score?"],[872.06,"Uh no. It's more like the Greek word there is more like headdress."],[878.22,"Okay. Some some sort of headress."],[879.66,"Some sort of headdress."],[880.74,"Not identical to Aaron's that has the plate and everything, but but some sort of headdress."],[882.06,"So notice again, we were talking about baptism"],[887.94,"Notice the process here."],[889.94,"Washed, dressed, anointed with oil."],[895.83,"And the anointing with oil here, and we're gonna see, they're gonna actually do this."],[900.47,"So this is all gonna get repeated in a few chapters."],[903.21,"All the things God's telling them to do now, we're gonna have to then hear about them doing."],[908.9,"So this will be repeated."],[911.3,"But the the anointing with oil here, the consecration of Aaron with the oil, is directly connected when it happens, especially with the Holy Spirit."],[923.24,"The idea is that now the Holy Spirit is going to empower Aaron, for his ministry."],[929.32,"And so remember in the Old Testament, there's not this idea that everybody is indwelt with the Holy Spirit."],[936.12,"That's post-Pentecost in the New Testament."],[938.98,"That's prophesied, like in Joel and the Old Testament, that the day will come when everyone will have the Holy Spirit."],[946.02,"But in the Old Testament, it's people like Moses, who we saw, remember, God took the spirit that was on Moses."],[952.5,"And um and uh Aaron as high priest, later on the king."],[959.23,"It's these people, these important positions who are empowered by the Holy Spirit to do their work."],[965.43,"Sometimes not even on a continual basis."],[968.19,"Sometimes it's just for a period of time to do something"],[972.15,"We'll see a lot of that in Joshua and Judges as we go forward."],[978.23,"But so that's connected here to that anointing, a la charismation."],[985.24,"Um so again, this is not something that's new in the New Testament or that's new, like post-New Testament that the church comes up with or evolves within the church somewhere."],[995.4,"This is going back to the Torah."],[998.14,"How do you do an ordination?"],[1001.18,"New Testament doesn't tell you how to do an ordination."],[1004.4,"St. Paul tells you the qualifications for deacons and presbyters and episcopy."],[1010.54,"He says, here's the requirements that you want."],[1011.1,"s. And he tell St"],[1014.5,"Timothy, when you go to Crete, or when you go to Ephesus, make um make presbyters everywhere you go."],[1024.61,"Find good men, make them presbyters, make them deacons."],[1027.95,"But you're gonna be hard pressed in the New Testament to find someplace where it tells you, okay, what was the the actual procedure?"],[1038.4,"And let me suggest that it's a tough argument, to me at least, that St."],[1043.92,"Paul just figured, oh, you know, do whatever you want."],[1049.25,"That's in terms of how you do it."],[1051.9,"Just come up with something."],[1052.94,"Paul knew the kind of things that a lot of the people he was writing to would come up with"],[1053.22,"Because St. Like whatever the Corinthians came up with would probably not be something he would endorse if they just made something up."],[1063.76,"But there's an assumption that, hey, you guys have all know the Torah, you all know the Hebrew scriptures, you all know how we do this."],[1071.65,"So do this."],[1073.89,"Yeah. I thought maybe like in the New Testament that it was the laying on of hands."],[1080.29,"Well, we're gonna see that too."],[1082.45,"Yeah. And that's gonna be part of it."],[1084.53,"But even that, just okay, lay hands in the street?"],[1090.37,"At their house."],[1092.07,"You know what I mean?"],[1092.95,"Like in terms of there's no description."],[1095.81,"And and that argues not that it doesn't matter what you do."],[1099.77,"You can do whatever you want."],[1100.97,"I think that's a hard argument to make."],[1104.09,"To me, that argues that this is something we're not changing."],[1109.3,"Because there were rabbinical ordinations in St."],[1112.98,"Paul's Day. Ordained as a rabbit."],[1115.52,"He had been yes, he had been through one."],[1117.86,"Like and had been to them."],[1119.48,"There there were rabbinical or there were there were, you know, and there were priests still in St."],[1123.88,"Paul's Day serving in the temple."],[1125.58,"Who were ordained."],[1126.87,"So he, you know, there were processes when you joined the Sanhedrin as one of the elders of the people."],[1133.05,"There were right religious ceremonies."],[1135.13,"So these these things already existed."],[1138.19,"So there are lots of places in the New Testament where there's a change because of Christ."],[1145.49,"And how things are done."],[1147.17,"But they're pretty much described in the New Testament."],[1151.43,"So you read Hebrews, Hebrews tells you why we don't get the two goats and do the Day of Atonement ritual anymore."],[1159.12,"It tells you why we don't do animal sacrifices for sin offerings anymore."],[1162.66,"There in Hebrews."],[1163.3,"It says, okay, here's a change."],[1165.0,"St. Paul has a lot of letters telling you why we don't practice circumcision anymore."],[1169.62,"So there's all these places where you could say, okay, well, no, see, here's, and and the New Testament explains in light of Christ why this change is happening."],[1181.18,"So it seems to me if something's just not mentioned, in the in the New Testament."],[1188.4,"Obviously, it's going on, but there's no specific description of it."],[1192.81,"That that the most reasonable, the most logical assumption to make is that they were continuing the then current Jewish practice."],[1202.75,"Not to assume, oh, now it just doesn't matter."],[1205.31,"You could, you know."],[1207.23,"So the Orthodox Church, that's how it's done."],[1209.79,"It's it's following the this raphetic nature that we see."],[1214.35,"Following what the Torah said, yeah."],[1215.87,"Is the Catholic Church like that too?"],[1217.63,"And their coordination and so there's been a lot of development and it varies a lot."],[1222.99,"Like you go to an Eastern Rite Roman Catholic Church, they do the exact same thing we do."],[1228.81,"Um but some things have changed."],[1232.17,"Some things have changed, especially because there are some things that in the Roman Catholic Church now only the Pope can do."],[1238.97,"Like only the Bishop of Rome can do them."],[1241.45,"Whereas for us, any bishop can do them."],[1245.5,"And I think most of them who who know what they're talking about in terms of church history would say, yeah, that's something that that was restricted to the Pope later, that earlier on, you know, that was done on a more widespread basis."],[1260.46,"They would acknowledge that that was a change or a development."],[1262.38,"They would say it's a good change and development."],[1264.96,"But they would acknowledge that it's a change or a development."],[1268.44,"Um not in the same identical way."],[1275.21,"But if you came to like the ordination of a priest in an Orthodox liturgy and you had this in front of you, you'd be like, oh, okay, yeah."],[1284.09,"Okay, yeah, okay, yeah."],[1286.3,"Um, you know, you would you would see the the direct connection."],[1293.58,"And I imagine in the Latin Rite, if you went to a traditional Latin Rite ordination, which I don't know if that even happens anymore in the Roman Catholic Church, but I imagine if you went to one of those, it would be pretty similar."],[1308.46,"I know in a lot of wa.ys"],[1309.74,"Yeah, yeah Yeah."],[1313.38,"Yeah. So I imagine the Catholics do that."],[1316.34,"Or at least have in the past."],[1318.1,"Yeah. It's Roman Catholicism now, it's hard to say what goes on in some places."],[1324.76,"And I wouldn't, and I'm not saying that to take a shot at the Roman Catholic Church."],[1328.92,"I'm saying that to try to be fair."],[1331.08,"Like, I don't expect every Roman Catholic to defend like every goofy thing that happens in a Roman Catholic Church somewhere."],[1339.5,"Like, because there are goofy things that go on in Orthodox, you know, an individual Orthodox Church, I'm being recorded, so I won't name any."],[1346.94,"But there there are there are some that are kind of infidimists where they do weird things."],[1351.36,"And if you ask any Orthodox apologist or any Orthodox pri.est or something about that, they'd be like, no, that doesn't represent what we do"],[1358.44,"So I'm saying the same thing's true with Roman Catholicism."],[1361.09,"And I don't want to try to hold them responsible for weird things that somebody somewhere is doing in the name of Roman Catholicism, because that's not fair."],[1370.53,"Um I try to go by mainstream practice, but yeah."],[1380.77,"Verse 10 You shall also have the young bull brought before the tabernacle of testimony, and Aaron and his sons shall put their hands on the head of the young bull before the Lord at the doors of the tabernacle of testimony."],[1392.81,"Then you shall kill the young bull before the Lord at the doors of the tabernacle of testimony."],[1397.57,"You shall take some of the blood of the young bull and and put it on the horns of the altar with your finger, pour all the blood beside the base of the altar."],[1405.85,"You shall take it on the ground."],[1407.63,"Yeah, yeah, at the ground at the base of the altar."],[1410.51,"Yeah. You shall take all the fat that covers the entrails, the fatty lobe attached to the liver, and the two kidneys, and the fat on them, and burn them on the altar."],[1420.35,"But the flesh of the young bull with its skin and offal, you shall burn with fire outside the camp, it is a sin offering."],[1431.11,"um So, we're doing this young bull first as a sin offering for Aaron and his sons."],[1437.27,"Okay? Now notice they lay hands on it."],[1442.27,"Do they say anything when they lay hands on it?"],[1448.72,"Nothing. They put their hands on it."],[1452.2,"And then it's killed."],[1453.48,"Okay, why am I emphasizing this?"],[1455.8,"Well, part of the problem with how we tend to view sacrifice, and this is especially in the Old Testament today, is that you will hear people say, people who should know better, say very offhandedly, well, when they did a sacrifice in the Old Testament, they would pronounce the sins of the person over the animal and then kill it."],[1480.36,"And so the animal was like taking the punishment or the death penalty for the person's sins."],[1486.21,"Does this say that?"],[1488.73,"No. So there is only one place in the Torah, in the Pedateuch, where sins are put on an animal."],[1498.86,"That's Leviticus chapter 16, the Day of Atonement."],[1502.22,"One of the goats."],[1503.44,"The goat that gets sent out into the wilderness."],[1505.84,"Notice what I just said."],[1507.52,"The goat that gets sent out into the wilderness, not the one that gets sacrificed."],[1514.02,"Once you put the sins on the goat, you can't sacrifice it."],[1517.86,"It's got sins all over it."],[1519.62,"It's unclean now."],[1522.98,"So you send it out."],[1524.68,"The other goat that's pure and clean or without blemish. that's the one that gets sacrificed in Leviticus 16"],[1534.24,"But nowhere else in the Old Testament, nowhere else in the Torah are sins put on an animal."],[1542.96,"Now, there are folks who will tell me to try to get around that point I just made."],[1549.04,"Because I've had people check me out on that."],[1550.88,"I've had people go through and read to try and find a place, and there aren't any."],[1555.52,"Try to say, well, you know, these other places where it talks about laying hands on the animal, it's just assuming that you're gonna read the sins over it."],[1566.16,"I'm like, okay."],[1567.44,"So let's say there's a hundred places where people lay hands on an animal before sacrificing it."],[1573.8,"99 of those are assuming that everyone knows that that means you're going to put the sins on it."],[1583.16,"And then this one time, they actually tell you to put the sins on it."],[1590.84,"Does that make sense?"],[1593.37,"That doesn't make sense as an argument."],[1595.53,"That it would be assumed 99% of the time and then enunciated once."],[1601.15,"If it was the other ratio."],[1603.84,"If it said that all the time and I found one place where it didn't, that would be a good argument to say to me, hey, you know what?"],[1611.1,"That one place it's probably assuming it."],[1614.64,"But it's not the reverse."],[1616.08,"Now, number two"],[1617.28,"So that was simply not the process."],[1620.72,"Immediately after this, notice it says kill the bull."],[1624.24,"How did they kill it?"],[1629.36,"What did they use?"],[1633.28,"Maybe. Some of the cultures of the time used as sacrificial axes."],[1639.12,"Could they have used an axe?"],[1642.64,"Okay. So, what's what's the importance of this?"],[1646.88,"Okay. Look at the detail that comes after that."],[1651.6,"Listing all the different parts of the animal that are gonna get burnt."],[1656.85,"Super level of detail."],[1658.39,"This is what you find. in sacrificial ritual descriptions"],[1663.47,"So the parts that are described in a lot of detail, those are the important parts of the ritual."],[1671.47,"That's why there's so much detail."],[1674.51,"These are the things you have to get right."],[1677.63,"These are the important parts of ritual that have to be done in a particular way because they have a particular symbolism, a particular significance."],[1685.29,"A particular meaning."],[1694.71,"That's the stuff that's not important."],[1699.44,"That's the stuff that matters less."],[1701.68,"So from this description, reading it, we would conclude that how exactly you kill the animal is not all that important to God."],[1713.24,"Whereas how you butcher it and how you offer it as a sacrifice is very important to God."],[1726.26,"For anyone reading itself is not the ritual."],[1736.54,"Again, we've been told all that sacrificing something is killing it."],[1741.38,"Killing is not important to the ritual, other than that, you know, they're not gonna, God's not gonna have them butcher the animal alive."],[1749.32,"So you've got to kill it like as a precursor to this."],[1752.78,"Before you butcher it and everything."],[1754.96,"You gotta kill it."],[1755.84,"But how exactly?"],[1759.36,"Not that important."],[1761.12,"But then what you do with the blood, what you do with the entrails, what you do with the meat portions, what do you do, that's very important."],[1767.76,"That's the ritual."],[1769.36,"Killing it is not the ritual, it's just a prerequisite."],[1778.64,"So that said, what is the significance of laying hands on something?"],[1786.8,"You actually brought it up earlier."],[1788.65,"Laying on of hands becomes part of ord.ination"],[1791.41,"Before we're out of the Torah, it's gonna be part of ordination."],[1795.83,"And what does that do?"],[1797.75,"That designates someone or something, for a particular purpose."],[1805.5,"And so that's what's going on when we see these laying on of hands."],[1811.36,"Before something is sacrificed."],[1813.92,"This bull. This young bull is being singled out for this purpose of being the sin offering."],[1821.29,"There's a lot of bulls."],[1823.15,"This one is the sin offering."],[1830.69,"So that's what's happening here in this ritual."],[1834.09,"And finally, since we have the word sin offering here, we're going to get into this a lot more in Leviticus, because Leviticus is going to give us a lot more detail on different types of sin offerings."],[1843.55,"But since the term sin offering is here."],[1846.49,"And you may have already picked this up based on what I already said, about this offering."],[1850.74,"What's going on here is not that this thing is being killed as a punishment for Aaron and his son's sins."],[1859.88,"Which doesn't really make sense anyway."],[1864.74,"Because presumably, at least I'd like to think they killed it in a relatively humane way."],[1870.16,"Like, why wouldn't they?"],[1871.98,"They didn't torture it."],[1872.7,"it. They didn't beat"],[1872.98,"They didn't like inflict suffering on it."],[1874.74,"They didn't punish it."],[1877.67,"Most likely, they either used an axe, and the axe is kind of uglier from our point of view."],[1882.87,"The sacrificial axes, they basically literally bury it in the animal's head."],[1887.91,"But that killed it."],[1890.31,"It's like the bolt guns they use to slaughter cattle now."],[1893.13,"Like it's whack, it's dead."],[1895.03,"Um, more likely it was a knife."],[1898.07,"Not 100% sure, but more likely it was a knife, and they would just slit its throat."],[1902.33,"And it died very quickly, and that helps them collect the blood."],[1906.41,"And drain the blood when they when they kill it that way."],[1910.46,"Interesting looking about this."],[1911.9,"There's a movie."],[1913.1,"It's been out for 10, 15 years now, I guess it's uh called the Nativity."],[1918.74,"And at some particular point in it, King Herod goes, they're priest there and King Herod goes up to the bowl and he puts his hands on the horns of the ball and his handle into the ball, and then the priest cut the throat of the bowl."],[1933.94,"Yeah. Yeah, because that's just the most efficient way."],[1937.5,"But how is the animal being punished?"],[1944.54,"Or how is it being punished more than any animal you kill and eat?"],[1949.88,"We don't normally think of it that way."],[1951.82,"I go out, kill a rabbit, clean it, my wife cooks it, serves it for dinner."],[1956.7,"Did I like punish the rabbit?"],[1960.1,"Unless I'm some kind of weirdo, no."],[1962.1,"Like I I went and I killed it."],[1964.1,"Probably by I mean, the way my grandpa used to do it is by slitting their throats."],[1968.16,"So you hang them up and you let the blood drain and then you"],[1971.77,"Th that's not punishment."],[1977.25,"And this idea that like so first of all you have to presume that somehow God imposes the death penalty for all sins, which isn't true already in the commandments you read in the Torah, there wasn't the death penalty for everything."],[1996.18,"But that God is like, okay, the person who does this has to die."],[1999.22,"die. Just something's gotta"],[2000.14,"But you know, I'll take a I'll take a goat."],[2006.94,"Does that make sense?"],[2013.52,"Like, it doesn't even make sense."],[2017.04,"Okay. So let's talk about how sin offerings actually worked."],[2021.54,"So as we've seen, there's this big focus on butchering it."],[2026.02,"And which parts of it go where, which are offered to God."],[2029.48,"In this case, we don't have a thing here that um uh anyone was eating this."],[2036.05,"This particular one."],[2037.17,"But that this is in an ordination."],[2038.93,"But in general, the priests would eat, priests would eat parts, and and the the the people who brought offering."],[2046.83,"Meaning it's this context of a meal."],[2049.84,"God doesn't literally eat his parts."],[2052.0,"They're burnt up."],[2053.7,"And the smoke goes up, and poetically that's described in the Old Testament as God says it's a sweet savor in his nostrils."],[2060.12,"The sacrifice. Sort of smell and taste being connected there."],[2064.85,"Alright. So this is a meal that the people are sharing with each other and with God."],[2077.41,"So when someone commits a sin."],[2083.55,"We haven't had all of them laid out yet, but we had the Ten Commandments."],[2085.63,"By the time we get here"],[2086.23,"We've had some other stuff."],[2089.17,"Whatever it is, what happens?"],[2091.25,"What functionally happens when you commit a sin?"],[2094.21,"Well, you have minimally broken a relationship with someone in your community."],[2101.81,"And also thereby broken your relationship with God."],[2108.63,"You have disrupted it."],[2109.91,"Because as we've already seen in the Torah, those two are the same thing."],[2113.75,"That's not new in the New Testament, the idea that loving God and loving your neighbor are the same thing."],[2118.89,"People get that."],[2119.85,"When Christ quotes the two commandments, love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, mind, and strength, and love your neighbor as yourself, those are from Deuteronomy and Leviticus."],[2128.26,"So he's quoting the Torah."],[2130.78,"So that's not a new idea."],[2135.42,"The Torah. So those two things have happened."],[2139.42,"And as we've seen already, even in the examples we have, what the quote unquote punishments are aimed at is not retributively punishing someone, making someone suffer for what they've done."],[2155.34,"They're aimed at reestablishing those relationships."],[2164.26,"Plus a little."],[2167.64,"So that you can restore the relationship with the person you stole from."],[2172.3,"So these are aimed at remediating the issue."],[2178.02,"And then once that issue has been dealt with."],[2182.82,"So my relationship with the people I sinned against, my relationship with the God I sinned against have been restored by me taking actions."],[2192.98,"By me repenting, not just feeling bad, but actually going and doing whatever I can do together."],[2209.2,"This is how sin offerings and peace offerings fundamentally work."],[2213.24,"Share this meal together to celebrate the fact that now. things have been restored"],[2220.56,"And this is something that later on in the old testament, we're going to see that the people didn't really understand."],[2231.12,"Because God's going to have to call them on it."],[2235.28,"God's going to have to call them on it repeatedly."],[2238.45,"And that's that they're going to think, oh no, if I do the magic sacrifice ritual correctly, God forgets about the bad things I did."],[2251.98,"And so I can do whatever I want."],[2254.7,"And then we just go do the magic ritual, and now we're cool with God"],[2259.5,"And. God's going to get to the point where he says, stop offering me sacrifices"],[2266.44,"I don't want them from you."],[2268.7,"He's going to say, I desire mercy, not sacrifice."],[2272.0,"Meaning, I want you to change how you're living and what you're doing, not just keep doing what you want and offer sacrifices."],[2278.82,"He's going to say he doesn't delight in the blood of bulls and goats."],[2281.63,"On and on and on."],[2283.92,"That the ritual is a way for them to participate in the forgiveness that's already happened, mediated through the community."],[2293.44,"So the reason this one is a little different, why we're not eating, is this is part of again the ordination."],[2302.1,"So the idea here is that this is about the purification and consecration of Aaron and his sons, who were just what?"],[2312.74,"Washed, dressed, anointed, and now this is the beginning of the sacrifices that are sort of capping off this celebration."],[2325.3,"So it's not that Aaron and his sons have been like repenting of their sins."],[2329.0,"That's going to be the normal sin offerings are about the celebration now of this new status that Aaron and his sons have, where they've been consecrated, meaning consecrated means made holy."],[2344.3,"They've been set apart, they've been made holy by God."],[2350.11,"So this is sin offering in the sense of offering for their previous sins."],[2356.11,"Yes, God has given them forgiveness."],[2357.71,"They're celebrating the fact that God has given them forgiveness of what they've done to this point and consecrated them and set them apart as holy."],[2366.03,"Now, in the future, they're gonna sin."],[2372.06,"And so part of their ministry is going to be to offer sacrifices for their own sins as well as those of the people."],[2378.94,"And they're gonna do the more regular sin offerings, and that's gonna involve their repentance and things."],[2384.84,"So this is sort of the initial one."],[2387.45,"Now think about this for a second. in terms of the parallel we already talked about with baptism and how baptism is practiced"],[2396.21,"And our understanding of baptism as being for the remission of sins."],[2403.21,"So baptism happens for the remission of sins."],[2405.85,"The person is washed."],[2408.51,"Made holy, consecrated, anointed."],[2412.96,"But just like Aaron and his sons, you know, we go out there and we still sin."],[2421.92,"And so then what happens when we sin in the future after that?"],[2426.16,"You go through the process of repentance."],[2429.34,"And restoration. So it's that same pattern."],[2430.8,"'s that same. It pattern"],[2434.73,"So they're sort of being given the gift of this new status and this new in the ordination."],[2443.42,"And so that's why this sin offering element is there."],[2448.38,"To emphasize that part of this is the forgiveness of their former, of their former sins."],[2456.16,"And this is actually, this is actually also still a part of how ordinations are done in the Orthodox Church."],[2462.08,"As part of the process of being ordained as a deacon or a priest, or being consecrated as a bishop, the person goes and gives a life confession."],[2472.98,"Of their whole life leading. up to it"],[2475.98,"Because there is this element within the ordination, it's not the same thing as baptism, but there's this element within the ordination of you are now being set apart for this new purpose."],[2486.7,"There is this break from your former life now."],[2491.36,"Where you're set apart to now embark on a new mode of living."],[2497.44,"With the awareness that, yeah, you're still gonna sin and you're still gonna need to engage in repentance going forward."],[2510.64,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God, Father Stephen.'s email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com"],[2523.32,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":24.0,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: where chapter 29 fits","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Picking up at <b>Exodus 29:1</b>; last time <b>ch. 28</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Before 28: <b>construction, design, implementation</b> of tabernacle</li><li class=\"d1\">structures, veils, everything involved</li><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 28 = <b>vestments</b> of high priest and priests - continuation</li><li class=\"d0\">All in context: <b>Moses on the mountain</b> talking to God</li><li class=\"d1\">design is <b>revealed</b>; he builds after the <b>pattern</b> he sees in God's presence</li><li class=\"d0\">This is what <b>Hebrews</b> points at: earthly things = <b>shadow</b> of heavenly sanctuary</li><li class=\"d1\">people call it \"Platonist thing\" - <b>no</b>; Exodus shows they are a <b>copy</b>, following the pattern</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrews points back to Exodus</li></ul>"},{"t":113.15,"lv":3,"h":"Tonight: consecrating the priests","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Logically follows the vestments</li><li class=\"d0\">God lays out <b>process for consecrating high priest and priests</b></li><li class=\"d1\">here <b>Aaron and his sons</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"We'll see how far we get\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Order of the description so far</li><li class=\"d1\">started with <b>Ark</b> and <b>most holy place</b></li><li class=\"d1\">worked out to the <b>outer courts</b></li><li class=\"d1\">then <b>vestments</b> of priests</li><li class=\"d1\">now <b>ordination / consecration</b> of priests</li></ul>"},{"t":196.28,"lv":2,"h":"Exodus 29:1-3 - Offerings for the ordination","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>One young bull</b>, <b>two rams</b> without blemish</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Unleavened loaves</b> kneaded with oil, <b>unleavened cakes</b> anointed with oil</li><li class=\"d1\">wheat flour, all in <b>one basket</b>, brought with the bull and rams</li><li class=\"d0\">Animals named → <b>a sacrifice</b> is coming</li><li class=\"d0\">Ordination takes place <b>within sacrificial ritual</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Sacramental things\" is <b>repetitious</b> - like saying \"ATM machine\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>sacrament</b> = sacred thing</li><li class=\"d0\">These sacred things are <b>joined with the sacrificial worship</b> at the center of Israel's relationship with her God</li><li class=\"d0\">Priests' job: make <b>sacrificial offerings</b> for themselves and the people</li><li class=\"d1\">offer the <b>incense</b>, the <b>animals</b>; also prepare and <b>butcher</b> them</li><li class=\"d0\">So ordination is tied to their <b>first sacrificial offering</b> - \"the core of what's going on\"</li></ul>"},{"t":350.1,"lv":2,"h":"Not all offerings are animals","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Point he'll make <b>all through Exodus and Leviticus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not all offerings are animals</li><li class=\"d1\">some animals: bull and two rams</li><li class=\"d1\">also <b>loaves and cakes</b></li><li class=\"d0\">True <b>across the ancient Near East</b>, ancient world in general</li><li class=\"d0\">Cakes vary by <b>available grain</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>wheat cakes</b> in Roman religion</li><li class=\"d1\">probably <b>rice cakes</b> in China - \"haven't made a huge study\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Constant: <b>offering breads, cakes, loaves</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Everything offered is <b>food</b> - but <b>not everything gets killed</b>, not everything has <b>blood</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Modern idea: <b>sacrificing = killing</b>, blood is the important part</li><li class=\"d1\">can't kill a wheat cake; <b>no blood</b> if you stab it</li><li class=\"d1\">in Leviticus many <b>sin offerings are wheat cake</b></li></ul>"},{"t":495.08,"lv":3,"h":"Blood and the shared meal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Except <b>Day of Atonement</b>, <b>nothing special</b> done with blood</li><li class=\"d1\">drained, <b>poured on the ground at base of the altar</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God says don't do anything with it - <b>pagans drank it</b>, did rituals with it</li><li class=\"d0\">Sacrifice is always <b>food</b>, three portions</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>best portion burned</b> - offered to God</li><li class=\"d1\">portion <b>eaten by the priests</b> - already seen in Exodus</li><li class=\"d1\">portion <b>eaten by the people</b> bringing the offering</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Passover</b> too: you actually <b>cook and eat</b> the lamb</li><li class=\"d0\">Reorienting from the \"bad contemporary thing we've been fed\"</li><li class=\"d1\">sacrifice is all about blood / killing things - \"pun not intended\"</li></ul>"},{"t":590.97,"lv":2,"h":"Why the Eucharist is a sacrifice","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Other Christians can't see why <b>Eucharist is a sacrifice</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"so you're killing Jesus again?\" → <b>sacrifices aren't about killing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Many sacrifices were <b>bread</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Thought experiment: <b>TARDIS</b> back, grab a random <b>ancient Near Eastern person</b>, have them watch the liturgy</li><li class=\"d1\">no English needed; wouldn't know details, <b>body and blood of Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">but would say: \"he was <b>sacrificing bread and wine to his God</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">An <b>Israelite</b> who knew Torah would say more: a <b>thank offering</b> with bread and wine</li><li class=\"d0\">No distinction: <b>animals = the sacrifices</b>, cakes = appetizers</li><li class=\"d0\">Greek term <i>trapezomata</i> = \"table things\" - used because it sounds better</li><li class=\"d1\">pagan temples had <b>banquet halls</b>; besides sacrificed meat, <b>appetizers, rolls, sides</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not</b> what this is - God specifies <b>three animals and two types of cakes</b></li></ul>"},{"t":747.58,"lv":2,"h":"29:4-9 - Washed, dressed, anointed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 4</b>: bring Aaron and sons to <b>door of the tabernacle</b>, <b>wash them with water</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 5</b>: dress Aaron: <b>cloak, ephod, full-length robe, oracle</b></li><li class=\"d1\">oracle = <b>breastplate</b> (Orthodox Study Bible's wording); attached on the ephod</li><li class=\"d1\">each <b>layer</b> from last time</li><li class=\"d0\">Washed, cleaned → <b>dressed</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Connection to <b>baptism</b>: baptized, immediately <b>dressed in white</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Connection to <b>ordination</b> in the Orthodox Church</li><li class=\"d1\">ordinand dressed with <b>pieces of the vestments by the bishop</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>vv. 6-9</b>: <b>mitre</b>, <b>golden plate</b> on it; <b>anointing oil poured on head</b></li><li class=\"d1\">sons: <b>robes, sashes, headdress</b> (Greek = \"headdress\", not turban; not identical to Aaron's, no plate)</li><li class=\"d1\">priesthood = <b>continual ordinance</b>; \"you shall consecrate Aaron and his sons\"</li></ul>"},{"t":887.94,"lv":3,"h":"Anointing and the Holy Spirit","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Pattern: <b>washed, dressed, anointed with oil</b></li><li class=\"d0\">All repeated in a few chapters - <b>told now, then hear them doing it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Anointing = directly connected with the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Spirit <b>empowers Aaron for his ministry</b></li><li class=\"d0\">OT: <b>not everyone</b> indwelt by the Spirit - that's <b>post-Pentecost</b></li><li class=\"d1\">prophesied in <b>Joel</b>: day when everyone has the Spirit</li><li class=\"d1\">OT: <b>Moses</b> (God took the Spirit that was on him), <b>high priest</b>, later <b>king</b></li><li class=\"d1\">important positions empowered; sometimes <b>only for a period</b> - lots in <b>Joshua and Judges</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Connected to anointing <i>a la</i> <b>chrismation</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not new in NT or evolved later - <b>goes back to the Torah</b></li></ul>"},{"t":998.14,"lv":2,"h":"Ordination in the New Testament","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"How do you do an ordination?\" - <b>NT doesn't say</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>St. Paul</b> gives <b>qualifications</b> for deacons, presbyters, bishops</li><li class=\"d1\">tells <b>Timothy</b> (Crete / Ephesus): find good men, make presbyters and deacons</li><li class=\"d1\">no description of the <b>actual procedure</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Tough to argue Paul figured \"just come up with something\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he knew what the <b>Corinthians</b> would come up with - not something he'd endorse</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>assumption</b>: you all know the Torah and Hebrew scriptures - <b>so do this</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: wasn't it the <b>laying on of hands</b>? A: yes, that's part of it - but NT gives no further detail</li><li class=\"d1\">argues this is something <b>we're not changing</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Rabbinical ordinations</b> in Paul's day (he'd been through one); <b>priests ordained</b>; process joining the <b>Sanhedrin</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Where Christ changes things, NT <b>explains why</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Hebrews</b>: no <b>two goats / Day of Atonement</b>, no animal <b>sin offerings</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's letters: no <b>circumcision</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Unmentioned → most logical: <b>continuing then-current Jewish practice</b>, not \"now it doesn't matter\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Orthodox Church follows this <b>typological</b> pattern, the Torah</li></ul>"},{"t":1215.87,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: Catholic ordination","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: is the <b>Catholic Church</b> like that too?</li><li class=\"d0\">A lot of <b>development</b>, varies a lot</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Eastern Rite</b> Catholic church: \"exact same thing we do\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Some things changed</li><li class=\"d1\">some things now <b>only the Pope</b> can do; for us <b>any bishop</b></li><li class=\"d1\">those who know church history would say <b>restricted to the Pope later</b> - a <b>development</b>, they'd call it a good one</li><li class=\"d0\">Orthodox visitor at a priest's ordination would see <b>direct connection</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Traditional <b>Latin Rite</b> ordination probably similar</li><li class=\"d0\">Current Roman Catholicism: hard to say what goes on in some places</li><li class=\"d1\">not a shot - trying to be <b>fair</b>; don't hold them to every \"goofy thing\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>goofy things happen in Orthodox parishes</b> too - \"I'm being recorded, so I won't name any\"</li><li class=\"d0\">He goes by <b>mainstream practice</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1380.77,"lv":2,"h":"29:10-14 - The young bull as sin offering","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 10</b>: bull brought before tabernacle; <b>Aaron and sons lay hands on its head</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 11</b>: <b>kill the bull</b> before the Lord at the door</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>vv. 12-13</b>: blood on <b>horns of the altar</b> with finger; <b>pour rest at base</b></li><li class=\"d1\">take <b>fat covering entrails</b>, <b>fatty lobe of liver</b>, <b>two kidneys</b> and their fat → burn on altar</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>v. 14</b>: flesh, skin, offal <b>burned outside the camp</b> - \"it is a <b>sin offering</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">First: bull as <b>sin offering for Aaron and his sons</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Note: they lay hands on it - <b>say anything?</b> → <b>nothing</b>; hands on, then killed</li></ul>"},{"t":1453.48,"lv":2,"h":"Sins are not laid on the animal","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Common claim, \"people who should know better\": pronounce <b>sins over the animal</b>, kill it</li><li class=\"d1\">animal takes <b>punishment / death penalty</b> for the sins</li><li class=\"d0\">Does the text say that? <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Only one place</b> in the Pentateuch where sins are put on an animal: <b>Leviticus 16</b>, Day of Atonement</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>goat sent into the wilderness</b> - <b>not</b> the one sacrificed</li><li class=\"d1\">sins on it → <b>unclean</b>, can't be sacrificed; so you send it out</li><li class=\"d1\">the other goat, <b>pure, without blemish</b>, is sacrificed</li><li class=\"d0\">Objection: other places \"assume\" you read the sins over it</li><li class=\"d1\">say 100 places lay hands on animals: <b>99 assume</b>, <b>1 spells it out</b>? Doesn't make sense</li><li class=\"d1\">reverse ratio would be a good argument; <b>not this one</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1620.72,"lv":3,"h":"Killing is not the ritual","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Kill the bull\" - <b>how</b>? No tool named</li><li class=\"d1\">some cultures used <b>sacrificial axes</b>; could have</li><li class=\"d0\">Then <b>super-detailed</b> list of parts to burn</li><li class=\"d1\">detailed parts = <b>important parts of the ritual</b>: particular <b>symbolism, significance, meaning</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Little detail = <b>matters less</b></li><li class=\"d0\">So <b>how you kill</b> isn't all that important to God</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>how you butcher and offer</b> it is very important</li><li class=\"d0\">Killing \"is not the ritual\" - just a <b>prerequisite</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God won't have them butcher it alive</li><li class=\"d0\">What matters: <b>blood, entrails, meat portions</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1778.64,"lv":3,"h":"Laying on of hands","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: significance of <b>laying hands</b>? Brought up earlier - <b>part of ordination</b> before we're out of the Torah</li><li class=\"d0\">It <b>designates</b> someone or something <b>for a particular purpose</b></li><li class=\"d0\">This young bull is <b>singled out as the sin offering</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"a lot of bulls\" - this one is the sin offering</li></ul>"},{"t":1834.09,"lv":2,"h":"Not punishment","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Leviticus</b> gives more detail on <b>types of sin offerings</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Here: not killed as <b>punishment for Aaron and sons' sins</b> - \"doesn't really make sense anyway\"</li><li class=\"d1\">presumably killed <b>humanely</b>; not tortured, beaten, punished</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>axe</b>: literally buried in the head - like <b>bolt guns</b> on cattle: \"whack, it's dead\"</li><li class=\"d1\">more likely a <b>knife</b>: slit throat, dies quickly, <b>blood drains</b> and is collected</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: a movie, <i>The Nativity Story</i> - Herod puts his hands on the bull's horns, priest <b>cuts the throat</b>; A: the most <b>efficient way</b></li><li class=\"d0\">How is it punished more than <b>any animal you kill and eat</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">rabbit: kill, clean, wife cooks it - did I punish the rabbit?</li><li class=\"d1\">grandpa <b>slit their throats</b>, hung them to drain - not punishment</li><li class=\"d0\">Assumes God imposes <b>death penalty for all sins</b> - <b>not true</b> even in the Torah's commandments</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I'll take a goat\" instead - \"doesn't even make sense\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2017.04,"lv":2,"h":"How sin offerings actually work","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Big focus: <b>butchering</b>, which parts go where, which <b>offered to God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">In general: <b>priests eat parts</b>, and <b>those who bring the offering</b></li><li class=\"d1\">a <b>meal</b>; God doesn't literally eat - burnt, <b>smoke goes up</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT poetry: <b>sweet savor in his nostrils</b> (smell and taste connected)</li><li class=\"d0\">A meal shared <b>with each other and with God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">When you sin: <b>break a relationship</b> with someone in the community</li><li class=\"d1\">thereby break relationship with <b>God</b> - <b>same thing</b> in the Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">loving God = loving neighbor <b>not new in NT</b>: Christ quotes <b>Deuteronomy and Leviticus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Punishments\" aim at <b>reestablishing relationships</b>, not retribution</li><li class=\"d1\">make restitution, <b>restore the relationship</b> with the person you stole from</li><li class=\"d0\">Then: relationship with people and God restored by <b>me repenting - actually acting</b>, not just feeling bad</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sin offerings and peace offerings</b>: share the meal to <b>celebrate that things are restored</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2220.56,"lv":3,"h":"Later: Israel misunderstands","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Later in the OT <b>people didn't understand</b> this; God <b>calls them on it repeatedly</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Their thinking: do the <b>magic sacrifice ritual</b> correctly, God forgets, <b>I can do whatever I want</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God: <b>stop offering me sacrifices</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I desire <b>mercy, not sacrifice</b>\" = change how you live</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't delight in <b>blood of bulls and goats</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ritual = way to <b>participate in the forgiveness already given</b>, mediated through the community</li></ul>"},{"t":2293.44,"lv":2,"h":"Why Aaron's sin offering is different","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not eaten here - part of the <b>ordination</b></li><li class=\"d0\">About <b>purification and consecration</b> of Aaron and sons: <b>washed, dressed, anointed</b>, now sacrifices <b>cap off the celebration</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not them <b>repenting</b> - normal sin offerings do that</li><li class=\"d1\">celebration of their <b>new status</b>: <b>consecrated = made holy</b>, set apart by God</li><li class=\"d0\">Sin offering for <b>previous sins</b>: God has <b>forgiven</b> them to this point and set them apart</li><li class=\"d0\">Future: they <b>will sin</b> - ministry includes offering for <b>their own sins</b> and the people's</li><li class=\"d1\">regular sin offerings involve <b>repentance</b></li><li class=\"d0\">This = the <b>initial one</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2384.84,"lv":2,"h":"Parallel with baptism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Baptism</b> = for <b>remission of sins</b>: washed, made holy, consecrated, anointed</li><li class=\"d0\">Like Aaron's sons, <b>we still sin</b> after</li><li class=\"d1\">then: <b>repentance and restoration</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Same pattern</b>: gift of new status → ongoing repentance</li><li class=\"d0\">Sin offering element <b>emphasizes forgiveness of former sins</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2456.16,"lv":3,"h":"Life confession at ordination","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Still part of ordination in the Orthodox Church</li><li class=\"d1\">deacon, priest, bishop: gives a <b>life confession</b> of whole life up to it</li><li class=\"d0\">Not same as baptism, but element of being <b>set apart for a new purpose</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A <b>break from your former life</b>; embark on a <b>new mode of living</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Aware you'll <b>still sin</b> and need <b>repentance</b> going forward</li></ul>"}]});
